<DocPageMeta Title="Project tour"
             Section="Get Started"
             Order="40"
             Status="DocPageStatus.Reviewed"
             TestedAgainst="2026.10.12.1"
             Description="What each project in a generated Shift Framework solution does, and how the pieces fit together."
             Keywords="project,structure,architecture,api,data,shared,web,functions,test,layers" />

<DocSection Title="Solution layout">
    <p>
        A Shift Framework project generated with default options is a four-project
        solution. Each project has a single, clear responsibility. The tree shows
        which project references which:
    </p>
    <pre><code>YourApp.API          ← ASP.NET Core host: the API, and it serves the Web app
 ├── YourApp.Web      ← Blazor WebAssembly frontend
 │    └── YourApp.Shared
 └── YourApp.Data     ← EF Core entities, repositories, mappers, migrations
      └── YourApp.Shared   ← DTOs, enums, action trees, localization</code></pre>

    <p>
        <b>Shared</b> is at the bottom: both <b>Web</b> and <b>Data</b> reference it,
        and it references nothing. That is what lets the API and the frontend share
        types without circular builds. <b>Web</b> and <b>Data</b> never reference each
        other — the browser only reaches the database through the API. <b>API</b> is at
        the top because it hosts everything: it references <b>Data</b> for the database
        and <b>Web</b> so it can serve the Blazor app.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you opted into <code>--addFunctions</code> or <code>--addTest</code>, you'll also
        see <code>YourApp.Functions</code> and <code>YourApp.Test</code> next to the API
        (see <b>Optional projects</b> below).
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="YourApp.Shared">
    <p>
        The smallest and most foundational project. Contains everything that needs
        to be visible to <b>both</b> the server and the Blazor client:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>DTOs</b> — usually two per entity: a view DTO used by the form (e.g. <code>ProductDTO</code>) and a list DTO used by the list page (<code>ProductListDTO</code>). These are the shapes that travel over the wire between the API and the frontend.</li>
        <li><b>Enums</b> — domain enums that show up in DTOs (the sample has <code>TrackingMethod</code>).</li>
        <li><b>Action trees</b> — TypeAuth permission definitions. The same tree drives the permission checks on the API endpoints and what the Blazor frontend shows (for example, which menu links a user sees), so a permission added in one place applies in both.</li>
        <li><b>Localization</b> — <code>.resx</code> resource files for the UI text (English plus Arabic in the sample).</li>
    </ul>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Note">
        <b>Shared has no behavior.</b> No services, no business logic, no database access.
        It's a contract project — pure data shapes. Treat it that way and your
        layering stays clean.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="YourApp.Data">
    <p>
        The persistence layer. Everything that touches Entity Framework Core lives here:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Entities</b> — the EF Core entity classes. Each one inherits from <code>ShiftEntity&lt;T&gt;</code>, which gives it an ID, audit fields (<code>CreateDate</code>, <code>LastSaveDate</code>, <code>CreatedByUserID</code>, <code>LastSavedByUserID</code>), soft delete (<code>IsDeleted</code>), and the <code>ReloadAfterSave</code> flag for free.</li>
        <li><b>DbContext</b> — <code>DbContext/DB.cs</code>, a class named <code>DB</code> that registers all entities. With <code>Internal</code> identity it inherits <code>ShiftIdentityDbContext</code>, which adds the identity tables; with <code>External</code> it inherits <code>ShiftDbContext</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Repositories</b> — one for each entity that needs its own behavior, inheriting <code>ShiftRepository&lt;DB, TEntity, TListDTO, TViewDTO&gt;</code>. The repository is where business rules live, not the endpoint. An endpoint can also use the framework's built-in repository instead of a class of its own — the sample's <code>api/country</code> endpoints do.</li>
        <li><b>Mappers</b> — translate between entities and DTOs. Most entities need nothing here at all: ShiftMapper declares the four maps of every entity/list-DTO/view-DTO triple at build time, from the repository's type arguments. This folder holds one mapper class for the project (<code>YourAppMapper.cs</code>, an ordinary <code>ShiftMapperBase</code>) where the members convention cannot fill are written, plus any fully hand-written <code>IShiftEntityMapper</code>.</li>
        <li><b>Migrations</b> — EF Core migration files. The folder starts empty; <MudLink Href="/get-started/run-the-sample">Run the generated sample</MudLink> shows how to create the first migration and apply it.</li>
        <li><b>Projections</b> / <b>Evaluators</b> — query-shaping helpers used by the sample repositories.</li>
        <li><b>ReplicationModels</b> — projections used by the optional Cosmos DB replication feature.</li>
    </ul>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="YourApp.API">
    <p>
        The ASP.NET Core host. This is what you actually run during development —
        it serves the API endpoints <b>and</b> hosts the Blazor WebAssembly bundle
        from <code>YourApp.Web</code>, so a single <code>dotnet run --project YourApp.API</code>
        gives you the full app.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Program.cs</b> — composition root. Wires up DI, ShiftIdentity, TypeAuth, Azure Storage, and the Blazor middleware. Its <code>RegisterShiftRepositories(...)</code> call also validates the mapping layer at startup — an entity/DTO triple with no mapper fails the boot rather than the first request. This is where you configure the app, not in scattered config files.</li>
        <li><b>Controllers</b> and <b>Endpoints</b> — the thin layer that exposes each entity over HTTP and runs the TypeAuth permission check. The sample shows three ways to do it: a controller (<code>InvoiceController</code>), minimal-API endpoints (<code>Endpoints/ProductEndpoints.cs</code>), and endpoints declared by attributes on the entity itself (<code>Country</code>'s <code>api/country</code>, served by the built-in repository). Business logic does <b>not</b> belong here.</li>
        <li><b>Services</b> — supporting services like <code>SendEmailService</code> (included with <code>Internal</code> identity). Anything that doesn't fit a repository pattern.</li>
        <li><b>Reports</b> — FastReport (.frx) report templates. The sample's <code>ProductCategory.frx</code> is filled and rendered by <code>ProductCategoryRepository</code> in <b>Data</b>.</li>
        <li><b>appsettings.Development.json</b> — connection strings, Azure Storage and Cosmos DB settings, identity configuration. It is the only settings file the template ships, so run the API in the <code>Development</code> environment.</li>
    </ul>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Tip">
        Always run <b>YourApp.API</b> for local development, not <code>YourApp.Web</code>
        directly. The API project is the host that includes the Blazor dev server
        and the backing endpoints the frontend calls.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="YourApp.Web">
    <p>
        The Blazor WebAssembly frontend. This is what users see in their browser
        once <code>YourApp.API</code> serves it.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Pages</b> — Blazor pages, one per route. List pages, form pages, dashboard pages — anything users navigate to.</li>
        <li><b>Components</b> — reusable Blazor components specific to your app (as opposed to the framework-provided ShiftBlazor components).</li>
        <li><b>Shared</b> — layout components (<code>MainLayout</code>, <code>NavMenu</code>) and other components shared across pages.</li>
        <li><b>Program.cs</b> — Blazor WASM composition root. Registers the <code>HttpClient</code> (with automatic token refresh), ShiftBlazor (which also registers MudBlazor), and any client-only services.</li>
        <li><b>wwwroot</b> — static assets: CSS, images, the <code>index.html</code> shell, and the client's <code>appsettings.Development.json</code> (API and identity URLs).</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        The frontend never talks to the database directly. Every interaction goes
        through the API, which means the same business rules and authorization
        enforced server-side automatically apply to the UI.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Optional projects">
    <h3>YourApp.Functions <small>(only if you passed <code>--addFunctions true</code>)</small></h3>
    <p>
        An Azure Functions project for code that doesn't fit the request/response
        pattern: timer triggers (scheduled jobs), queue/blob handlers, integration
        webhooks. Pre-wired with TypeAuth so the same permission checks you use in
        the API also work inside Functions.
    </p>

    <h3>YourApp.Test <small>(only if you passed <code>--addTest true</code>)</small></h3>
    <p>
        An xUnit integration test project that starts your API in-process and
        exercises your repositories and endpoints end-to-end against a real SQL Server
        database. It needs SQL Server Express at <code>localhost\sqlexpress</code> — see
        <MudLink Href="/get-started/template-parameters">Template parameters</MudLink>.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="The flow of a single request">
    <p>
        To make the layering concrete, here's what happens when a user clicks
        "Save" on a product form in the generated sample:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>The Blazor form in <b>Web</b> serializes the <code>ProductDTO</code> (defined in <b>Shared</b>) and sends it to <code>api/product</code> — a POST for a new product, a PUT for an existing one.</li>
        <li>The product endpoints in <b>API</b> (<code>Endpoints/ProductEndpoints.cs</code>, mapped with <code>MapShiftEntitySecureCrud</code>) receive the DTO, run the TypeAuth permission check for the <code>Product</code> action from the action tree in <b>Shared</b>, and hand the DTO to <code>ProductRepository</code>.</li>
        <li><code>ProductRepository</code> in <b>Data</b> maps the DTO onto a <code>Product</code> entity, applies business rules, and saves via the DbContext.</li>
        <li>The saved entity is mapped back into a <code>ProductDTO</code>, which the endpoint returns as JSON.</li>
        <li>Blazor receives the response, updates its state, and shows a success message.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        Notice that the endpoint is essentially a glue layer: it checks the permission
        and calls the repository. The repository owns the business logic and the DTO
        contract is the only thing that crosses layer boundaries. This is the rhythm
        you'll repeat for every entity.
    </p>
</DocSection>

